+/**
+ * The obligations of the dedicated token-endpoint renewal operation that a COMPILING IMPLEMENTATION does not
+ * discharge: the registration hop, the published capability metadata, the refusal invariant, the kind of
+ * error an unreadable credential produces, and the two conservation properties a tree that changed nothing
+ * already satisfies.
+ * <p>
+ * This grades a strict subset of the axes of the ten-axis contract beside it, and the axis numbers are
+ * inherited from that file deliberately: the anchors of the note wave were measured per axis, so a reading
+ * of this file is directly comparable with that table. A1, A3, A6 and A8 are absent by design — the no-note
+ * solver satisfied every one of them in every replicate, so they measure whether an implementation was
+ * written, not whether the architecture was understood.
+ * <p>
+ * Every {@code @Test} below is meant to be <em>independently</em> satisfiable, so that the number of passing
+ * tests measures how much of the work is still missing rather than acting as one boolean wearing six names.
+ * In particular the behaviour axes (A7 and A9) never consult the ServiceLoader registration, the
+ * registration axis (A2) never requires the compiled-class scan to have found anything, and the uniqueness
+ * axis (A4) does not require the grant to be registered at all.
+ * <p>
+ * The implementation under test is never referenced by class name, nor by the grant-type identifier or the
+ * short code it happens to choose. It is discovered by the union of two name-independent routes:
+ * <ol>
+ *     <li>a scan of the compiled classes directory of this module for concrete {@link OAuth2GrantType} and
+ *     {@link OAuth2GrantTypeFactory} implementations under {@code org/keycloak/protocol/oidc/grants/} that
+ *     are absent from the baseline measured on the pristine tree and pinned below;</li>
+ *     <li>the provider SPI: {@link OAuth2GrantType} factory ids resolvable through the session factory that
+ *     are absent from the baseline measured on the pristine tree and pinned below.</li>
+ * </ol>
+ * When neither route finds anything, the behaviour axes FAIL with an explanatory message; they never ERROR.
+ * <p>
+ * Every behaviour axis exercises the grant in the state the runtime puts it in. {@code TokenEndpoint}
+ * authenticates the client, builds the CORS context, stamps the event type
+ * ({@code event.event(grant.getEventType())}) and only then constructs the {@link OAuth2GrantType.Context}
+ * and calls {@code grant.process(context)}. A grant that has never had its event type stamped is a state the
+ * server never creates - {@link EventBuilder#error(String)} throws {@link IllegalStateException} in it - and
+ * punishing an implementation for it would grade the harness, not the code. The whole set-up therefore lives
+ * in exactly one place, {@link #invokeWith(String)}, so no axis added later can forget it.
+ */
